TzD (task #42): larger kernel stacks while TzD frames are large.
TzD's bounds checks and 24-byte pointer records make stack frames
larger, as KASAN's instrumentation does, and the 16 KiB task stack
overflowed (kthreadd: copy_process -> __schedule -> rb_erase).  The
kernel already doubles its stacks for KASAN through KASAN_STACK_ORDER;
TZD_STACK_ORDER, passed by tests/kernel/build_tzd.sh in KCPPFLAGS (seen
by C, assembly and the linker script), does the same.  Without it the
kernel is unchanged.  Interim: smaller frames are the real fix.
--- a/arch/x86/include/asm/page_64_types.h	2026-11-13 18:25:47.534315269 +0900
+++ b/arch/x86/include/asm/page_64_types.h	2026-11-13 18:25:47.554969554 +0900
@@ -8,6 +8,11 @@
 
 #ifdef CONFIG_KASAN
 #define KASAN_STACK_ORDER 1
+#elif defined(TZD_STACK_ORDER)
+/* TzD (task #42): its checks and 24-byte pointer records make frames
+   larger, as KASAN's instrumentation does; tests/kernel/build_tzd.sh
+   passes TZD_STACK_ORDER (C, assembly and the linker script alike).  */
+#define KASAN_STACK_ORDER TZD_STACK_ORDER
 #else
 #define KASAN_STACK_ORDER 0
 #endif
